# Hosting from your own PC, with no domain to buy (compose "tls" profile).
#
# Students open https://<your-ip-with-dashes>.sslip.io. sslip.io is a free
# public DNS service: the name 203-0-113-5.sslip.io resolves to 203.0.113.5, so
# Caddy can get a real Let's Encrypt certificate for it. A bare IP would not
# work: logins need HTTPS, and normal certificates are issued for names.
#
# Steps
#  1. Check that your router has a real public IP. Compare the router's WAN IP
#     (in its admin page) with https://ifconfig.me. If they differ, or the WAN
#     IP starts with 10., 192.168., 172.16-31. or 100.64-127., your provider
#     shares one IP across customers (CGNAT) and port forwarding cannot work.
#     Use a tunnel instead (see "Behind CGNAT" below).
#  2. Give this PC a fixed local IP in the router (DHCP reservation),
#     e.g. 192.168.1.50.
#  3. Forward these router ports to that local IP:
#       TCP 80   (Let's Encrypt checks it, then it redirects to HTTPS)
#       TCP 443  (the app)
#       UDP 443  (optional, HTTP/3)
#  4. Allow ports 80 and 443 in the Windows firewall (Docker Desktop usually
#     asks the first time).
#  5. Copy this file to .env, then set POSTGRES_PASSWORD and replace
#     203-0-113-5 with your public IP, dashes instead of dots, in both lines.
#  6. Start, or restart after changing .env:
#       docker compose --profile tls up -d --build
#     Wait ~30 s for the certificate; "docker compose logs caddy" shows
#     "certificate obtained successfully".
#  7. Create the first admin (PowerShell; the password is read from stdin):
#       'a-strong-password' | docker compose exec -T app /app/server create-admin you@school.edu "Your Name"
#  8. Log in at the same address students use, https://203-0-113-5.sslip.io,
#     not at localhost: the app only accepts logins from QP_BASE_URL.
#
# Check from a phone on mobile data (not your Wi-Fi). Some routers can't loop
# back to their own public IP, so that address may fail from inside your
# network but work from outside.
#
# When your public IP changes (after a router restart, for example), update
# both lines below, run step 6 again and share the new link. A free dynamic DNS
# name (e.g. DuckDNS) follows the IP by itself; use it in place of the
# sslip.io name.
#
# Turn the port forwards off when you're not hosting. Your PC must stay on
# with Docker running during class.
#
# Behind CGNAT, or no access to the router: a free Cloudflare quick tunnel
# gives an HTTPS address with no router changes and no Caddy:
#     cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:8080
# Set QP_BASE_URL to the https://….trycloudflare.com address it prints, run
# "docker compose up -d" (without the tls profile) and share that address.
# It changes each time the tunnel starts.

# Required: password of the PostgreSQL role "quiz".
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me

# Your public IP with dashes, as an sslip.io name (or your dynamic DNS name).
QP_DOMAIN=203-0-113-5.sslip.io
# Must be exactly the address students and you open.
QP_BASE_URL=https://203-0-113-5.sslip.io

# The app's own port stays on this PC; Caddy is the only public entry point.
QP_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1:8080
CADDY_HTTP_PORT=80
CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=443

# Swagger UI at /api/docs; off when the app is on the internet.
QP_API_DOCS=0
